WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:16.586
Unit sixteen: Qualitative Research Methods. Today you will contrast approaches with 'compared to' and 'in contrast to'.

4
00:00:16.886 --> 00:00:19.399
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.624
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.426
Interview.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:30.285
Interview means a structured or open conversation used to gather data.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:34.206
Semi-structured interviews allow follow-up questions.

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.202
Your turn. Say: Interview.

10
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:40.975
Interview.

11
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:42.857
Case study.

12
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.230
Case study means an in-depth investigation of a single person, group or event.

13
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:51.966
The case study examined one school over five years.

14
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.028
Your turn. Say: Case study.

15
00:00:57.867 --> 00:00:58.914
Case study.

16
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:00.465
Coding.

17
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.226
Coding means labelling segments of qualitative data by concept.

18
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:08.606
Coding revealed patterns invisible on a first reading.

19
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:11.846
Your turn. Say: Coding.

20
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.597
Coding.

21
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:16.980
Theme.

22
00:01:17.983 --> 00:01:20.888
Theme means a recurring pattern of meaning across data.

23
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:23.878
Isolation emerged as a dominant theme.

24
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:27.089
Your turn. Say: Theme.

25
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:30.679
Theme.

26
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:32.406
Saturation.

27
00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:37.541
Saturation means the point at which further data yields no new insights.

28
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.009
Interviews continued until saturation was reached.

29
00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:44.617
Your turn. Say: Saturation.

30
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:48.781
Saturation.

31
00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:50.773
Triangulation.

32
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:56.325
Triangulation means using multiple sources or methods to confirm findings.

33
00:01:56.917 --> 00:02:00.112
Triangulation with observation strengthened the conclusions.

34
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:03.741
Your turn. Say: Triangulation.

35
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.822
Triangulation.

36
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:09.445
Narrative.

37
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.272
Narrative means a participant's account of their experience.

38
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:17.347
Each narrative revealed a different motivation.

39
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.440
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

40
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.429
Narrative.

41
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:26.312
Context.

42
00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:30.698
Context means the circumstances that give meaning to behaviour.

43
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.502
Findings must be interpreted in their cultural context.

44
00:02:35.117 --> 00:02:38.535
Your turn. Say: Context.

45
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.552
Context.

46
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:47.122
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:52.839
Visal, why did you choose interviews rather than a questionnaire for your study of migrant workers?

48
00:02:53.950 --> 00:03:01.931
Compared to surveys, interviews reveal richer context. I wanted to understand why people leave, not just how many.

49
00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:05.353
How did you make sense of so much text?

50
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:14.129
Through coding. In contrast to statistical analysis, the themes emerged gradually from the data itself.

51
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:17.367
How did you know when to stop interviewing?

52
00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:24.085
When I reached saturation. By the fifteenth interview, no new themes were appearing.

53
00:03:25.233 --> 00:03:26.536
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.383 --> 00:03:30.857
Grammar focus: Compared to and in contrast to.

55
00:03:31.157 --> 00:03:32.286
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:36.469
Compared to surveys, interviews reveal richer context.

57
00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:41.960
In contrast to statistical analysis, coding lets themes emerge gradually.

58
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.485
Unlike large-scale studies, a case study prioritises depth over breadth.

59
00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:57.007
Place the contrast phrase first, followed by a comma. Make sure you compare like with like: 'Compared to surveys, interviews …', not 'Compared to surveys, I …'.

60
00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:02.306
Be careful! Don't say: In contrast of surveys, interviews go deeper.

61
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:07.381
Say: In contrast to surveys, interviews go deeper.

62
00:04:07.681 --> 00:04:09.564
The phrase is 'in contrast to'.

63
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:13.019
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:15.357
The Why Behind the What.

65
00:04:15.657 --> 00:04:20.176
Qualitative research seeks to understand experiences, meanings and motivations.

66
00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:27.590
Compared to surveys, interviews reveal richer context, because participants can explain their reasoning in their own words.

67
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.490
Researchers code transcripts so that recurring themes gradually emerge from the data.

68
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:40.528
In contrast to statistical studies, samples are usually small, and generalisation is not the primary goal.

69
00:04:41.533 --> 00:04:46.795
Triangulation with observation or documents helps to confirm that interpretations are credible.

70
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.832
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.683 --> 00:04:55.577
blank to surveys, interviews reveal richer context.

72
00:04:55.877 --> 00:04:59.263
A, Compare. B, Compared. C, Comparing.

73
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.556
The answer is B: Compared.

74
00:05:05.717 --> 00:05:07.693
Which sentence compares like with like?

75
00:05:07.993 --> 00:05:16.302
A, Compared to surveys, I prefer interviews.. B, Compared to surveys, interviews are richer.. C, Compared to surveys, the researcher is richer..

76
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:23.677
The answer is B: Compared to surveys, interviews are richer..

77
00:05:24.817 --> 00:05:28.209
The point at which new data yields no new insights is blank.

78
00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:32.650
A, triangulation. B, saturation. C, coding.

79
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.548
The answer is B: saturation.

80
00:05:39.683 --> 00:05:42.809
Labelling segments of data by concept is called blank.

81
00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:46.449
A, coding. B, sampling. C, regression.

82
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.698
The answer is A: coding.

83
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.835
According to the reading, what is NOT the primary goal of qualitative research?

84
00:05:57.135 --> 00:06:02.274
A, understanding meanings. B, generalisation. C, exploring motivation.

85
00:06:05.833 --> 00:06:08.657
The answer is B: generalisation.

86
00:06:09.867 --> 00:06:13.146
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.983 --> 00:06:17.690
What is one strength of qualitative research compared to quantitative?

88
00:06:34.483 --> 00:06:35.844
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.144 --> 00:07:11.243
Compared to quantitative research, qualitative research is much better at explaining why people behave as they do. Numbers might show that many students drop out, but only interviews reveal the personal and cultural reasons behind those decisions. For instance, a student might leave school to support family members, which a survey could easily miss. The obvious limitation is that small samples are difficult to generalise. Even so, both approaches are complementary rather than competing.

90
00:07:11.543 --> 00:07:13.229
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.117 --> 00:07:18.778
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and compared to and in contrast to.

92
00:07:19.078 --> 00:07:24.087
Today's value is depth of understanding. Look beneath the surface to understand people truly.

93
00:07:24.387 --> 00:07:27.283
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.583 --> 00:07:30.734
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

